const body = `<h1>9.6b — BattleEngine: One Loop, Infinite Monsters</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Payoff</h3>

    <p>You have three different creature types, each with unique combat behavior.
    But your combat code shouldn't care which one it's fighting. This is polymorphism:
    write one battle loop that works with ANY Combatant — now and forever.</p>

    <p>Open the <a href="../docs/unit-09/character-hierarchy.html">Combat System
    (Student RPG Guide)</a>. Follow Part 2: BattleEngine.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build: BattleEngine.java</h3>
        <p>Create <code>BattleEngine.java</code>. ONE static method:</p>
        <pre><code>public static Combatant runBattle(Combatant a, Combatant b) {
    System.out.println("=== COMBAT: " + a.getName() + " vs " + b.getName() + " ===");

    while (a.isAlive() && b.isAlive()) {
        System.out.println(a.getName() + " [" + a.getHp() + "] vs "
                         + b.getName() + " [" + b.getHp() + "]");

        // A attacks B
        int dmg = a.attack();
        System.out.println(a.getName() + " deals " + dmg + " damage!");
        b.takeDamage(dmg);

        // B counterattacks (if still alive)
        if (b.isAlive()) {
            dmg = b.attack();
            System.out.println(b.getName() + " deals " + dmg + " damage!");
            a.takeDamage(dmg);
        }
        System.out.println();  // blank line between rounds
    }

    Combatant winner = a.isAlive() ? a : b;
    System.out.println(winner.getName() + " wins!");
    return winner;
}</code></pre>

        <p>This method knows NOTHING about Goblin, Dragon, or Skeleton. It works
        with ANY two Combatants. Test it:</p>
        <pre><code>Combatant goblin = new Goblin();
Combatant dragon = new Dragon();
Combatant winner = BattleEngine.runBattle(goblin, dragon);</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The engine calls <code>a.attack()</code>. If a is a Skeleton,
            that does one thing. If a is a Dragon, it does another. How does
            Java know which one to call? What is the name of this mechanism?</li>
            <li>Could you add a new creature type — say, Healer that restores HP
            on attack — without changing a single line of BattleEngine? Why or
            why not?</li>
            <li>What happens if you pass null for either parameter? Where should
            you add a null check?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Acceptance — your BattleEngine must:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Work with any two Combatant implementations</li>
            <li>Handle Goblin vs Dragon, Skeleton vs Player, Player vs Player</li>
            <li>Return the winning Combatant</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/character-hierarchy.html">Combat System (Student RPG Guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/polymorphism.html">Polymorphism</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
